### M-EW-007 — Vision of a veiled, bent man in the garden (Thérèse, Lisieux, c. 1879)
- Period: during life
- What was reported: At about 6–7, at about 2 pm, while her father was away for several days, Thérèse at a window saw in the garden, about 20 m away, a figure with her father's height and bearing, but bent as if by age, head covered by a veil of uncertain colour. She called "Papa, papa". Marie and Pauline ran from the next room. They searched the walled garden and found no trace. They told her not to think or speak of it. She stayed convinced it announced a trial; she later linked it to her father's mental decline.
- The person helped: none (premonition)
- Witnesses and reporter: Mère Agnès, present right after; Marie
- Status: sworn testimony at the Ordinary Process; not an approved miracle
- Source: PO 1, f. 233r–v, Session 17, 31 Aug 1910
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: Pauline says Thérèse was calm, balanced, "not imaginative"; the vision came three years before her childhood illness.
- Doubts / other versions: Told in MS A. The judge asked whether the sad meaning was only claimed after the father's illness; Pauline said Thérèse believed from the start it foretold something painful.
- Extraction ID: A-M01

### M-EW-008 — Prophetic vision of her father (c. 1879)
- Period: during life
- What was reported: About age six Thérèse saw her father, then away, walking in the garden bent with age and head covered with a kind of veil. Marie heard her call "Papa, papa". Ten years later, in the first attacks of his cerebral illness, Louis covered his head with his handkerchief.
- The person helped: none (prophecy)
- Witnesses and reporter: Marie heard the cry; Céline also testifies to the handkerchief
- Status: sworn testimony; interpretation by the family
- Source: PO 3, f. 322v–323r; PO 4, f. 394v–395r
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: Louis's "paralysie cérébrale"
- Doubts / other versions: Marie dates the vision 1879, "ten years before the event".
- Extraction ID: C-M03

### M-EW-009 — Vision of her father veiled (Thérèse, Buissonnets, c. 1879–80)
- Period: during life
- What was reported: At about seven she saw through a window her father, then away for several days, walking at the bottom of the garden, head covered with a dark cloth, bent with age. Marie and Pauline heard her cry "Papa, papa" in anguish, took her into the garden to show no one was there; she remained sure. At the start of his paralysis (1889) Louis often covered his head.
- The person helped: —
- Witnesses and reporter: Marie (heard the cry, present); Céline (absent, heard it from her sisters)
- Status: sworn testimony; treated as prophetic vision; not a miracle
