- Doubts / other versions: Marie says the meaning was understood when Thérèse retold it on a recreation day in Carmel; Céline says it was revealed only after their father's death (1894). Marie: "dark cloth"; Céline: "thick veil".
- Extraction ID: D-M04

### M-EW-010 — Pierre Derrien, Carmel sacristan: illness, voice, telegram
- Period: after death
- What was reported: A Breton postal courier (b. 1880) ill three years with acute bronchitis, typhoid, and muco-membranous enteritis heard an inner voice: "Pierre, I am looking for souls for Jesus." On 21 March 1911 the voice said a full cure would make him offend God again. He accepted suffering, then recovered gradually without remedies. At the tomb in March 1913 the voice said "ask to come into my service"; the Carmel at first had no need. After blood-spitting on a Rome pilgrimage (June 1913) he recovered after a novena. On 29 Sept 1913 at 4 p.m. a telegram from the prioress asked if he would become "little Thérèse's servant". He became sacristan of the Carmel chapel. He also found coins on the road when he wanted to help the poor.
- The person helped: Pierre-Marie Derrien
- Witnesses and reporter: himself
- Status: co-witness ex officio at the Apostolic Process, 29–30 Sept 1916, pp. 1313–1325; not approved miracles
- Source: PA-25 file, co-witness 2, [1314–1324]
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: bronchitis, typhoid, enteritis; haemoptysis; he stopped all medicines
- Doubts / other versions: he was the Carmel's own employee when testifying
- Extraction ID: G-M16

### M-EW-011 — Cure of Thérèse by the Virgin's smile (Buissonnets, May 1883)
- Period: during life
- What was reported: After the "poison" crisis, Marie knelt with her sisters before the statue of the Virgin and prayed three times. At the third time Thérèse stared at the statue, her look radiant "as in ecstasy", for four to five minutes; two tears fell; then she looked at Marie tenderly. From that moment no trace of the illness remained. She told Marie she had seen the Virgin herself.
- The person helped: Thérèse, aged 10
- Witnesses and reporter: Marie (eyewitness); Léonie and Céline present
- Status: sworn testimony at the Ordinary Process; not an approved canonization miracle
- Source: PO 3, f. 311v–312r and 323r, S23 and S25
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: hallucinations, falls from bed, head-banging, loss of voice, showers prescribed; doctor said "science is powerless"
- Doubts / other versions: Céline dates the cure 10 May (usual date 13 May 1883). Marie calls the illness diabolical; Céline notes religious objects never frightened her, unlike usual demonic cases.
- Extraction ID: C-M01

### M-EW-012 — Cure by the Virgin's smile (Thérèse, Buissonnets, 10 May 1883)
- Period: during life
- What was reported: After weeks of terrors, convulsive movements and delirium, Marie, Léonie and Céline prayed before the statue of the Virgin. At Marie's third prayer Thérèse gazed at the statue with a radiant, ecstatic look for 4–5 minutes, shed two large tears, and was cured instantly and permanently. She told Marie she wept "because I could not see her any more". In 1897 she said: today it is the statue; then it was not the statue.
- The person helped: Thérèse, aged 10
- Witnesses and reporter: Marie (present, led the prayer); Céline (present, then 13–14: "I had no doubt the Virgin had appeared to her"); Dr Notta, who said "not hysteria"
- Status: sworn testimony at the Apostolic Process; not one of the approved canonisation miracles
- Source: PA 7, [562]–[565], [604], [615], Sessions 22, 25, 26; PA 8, [637]–[638], [777], Sessions 27, 37
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: daily showers prescribed; hallucinations; head-banging; muteness; about six weeks; no relapse; "science powerless"
- Doubts / other versions: Marie and Céline both call the illness diabolic in origin. Céline suggests grief at losing her "mothers" to Carmel may have contributed.
- Extraction ID: D-M02

### M-EW-013 — The childhood illness and the smile of the Virgin (Thérèse, Lisieux, 13 May 1883)
- Period: during life
- What was reported: At 10½ she had crises of terror, horrible visions and an impulse to throw herself from bed head-first onto the floor. She later said she never lost her reason and heard everything. She believed the devil caused it. During a novena to Notre-Dame des Victoires, in a worse crisis, her sisters prayed before a statue in the room; she saw the statue come alive, approach and smile. The illness vanished for good.
- The person helped: Thérèse
- Witnesses and reporter: Mère Agnès reports; she was in Carmel and knew it from the parlour and from Thérèse
- Status: sworn testimony; not an approved miracle
- Source: PO 1, f. 234r–v, Session 17, 31 Aug 1910
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: "passing" illness, left no trace
- Doubts / other versions: Pauline gives the date as 10 May 1883; the usual date is 13 May 1883 (Pentecost). Pauline defers to her sisters for details. In the infirmary in 1897 Pauline placed the same statue near her; Thérèse said it never seemed so beautiful, "but today it's the statue; before, you know it wasn't the statue" (f. 234v).
- Extraction ID: A-M02

### M-EW-014 — Sr Jeanne-Marie de l'Enfant-Jésus (Jeanne-Marie Halgand): graces, perfume, filled box, filled kettle
- Period: after death
- What was reported: A lay sister of Lisieux Carmel (b. 1886 at Crossac, fisherman's daughter; entered 11 Nov 1905) testified that: a look at Thérèse's image gave her strength in scruples and temptations against faith and certainty she would enter Carmel; the temptations ended on her clothing day (15 Oct 1906). In Oct 1907, alone in the kitchen for about two weeks, she did three people's work without fatigue and every morning found her cell smelling of incense. In 1909 an inner voice spoke to her about prayer. In Christmas week 1910, preparing 900–1,000 relic "authentiques" for the prioress's feast with only about 100, she found the box full at 3 p.m.; the prioress believed it; she smelled violets three times. On 23 Feb 1910 a kitchen kettle holding four jugs was found full after she poured one; Sr Marie-Madeleine confirms she had not filled it. On 10 May 1912 Thérèse's voice woke her urging a sacrifice.
- The person helped: Sr Jeanne-Marie de l'Enfant-Jésus OCD
- Witnesses and reporter: herself; Sr Marie-Madeleine (kettle); the prioress (box)
- Status: co-witness ex officio at the Apostolic Process, 29 Sept 1916, pp. 1307–1313; not approved miracles
- Source: PA-25 file, co-witness 1, [1308–1312]
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: scruples, temptations against faith
- Doubts / other versions: she herself went to check with the prioress for fear of illusion; the kettle had been emptied and cleaned just before
- Extraction ID: G-M15

