### M-EW-025 — The broken sandal (Marie de l'Eucharistie, winter 1897–98)
- Period: after death
- What was reported: Marie de l'Eucharistie, with frozen feet, chose to mortify herself and not put on her warm alpargates (rope-soled sandals), which she had permission to wear because of nosebleeds. As she passed the room where they dried, one of her cold sandals broke beyond repair, so she had to put on the warm ones. She saw it as one of "my little Thérèse's tricks".
- The person helped: Sr Marie de l'Eucharistie (Marie Guérin)
- Witnesses and reporter: her own letter to her father, 3 July 1898, filed by Jeanne
- Status: family letter in sworn testimony at the Ordinary Process; a "little grace", not a miracle claim
- Source: PO-20 [1154v–1155r]
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: frequent nosebleeds; rope soles soak up damp; foot-warmers forbidden for warming feet in Carmel
- Doubts / other versions: a natural event read as a sign
- Note: The letter adds that Thérèse, as novice guide, was "always full of tenderness" and often stopped her novices from suffering, then let them sacrifice and found ways to lessen it.
