### M-EW-079 — Abbé Weber's sight restored from cataract (Saint-Jean-de-Luz, 1910)
- Period: after death
- What was reported: The Carmel of Zarauz made three novenas to Thérèse (March, May, June 1910) for Weber's sight. Weber had never heard of her. A few days into the first, he was surprised to read easily and see the altar steps. After the second (begun Thursday 19 May 1910, with a picture-relic laid on his eyes each evening), he could read the tiny print of the Decrees of the Congregation of Rites at the front of his breviary and recognize people at over a hundred paces. During the third, he could again see bee eggs in a queen cell with the naked eye. He said his sight was back to its state before 1899, better than normal for his age (76).
- The person helped: Abbé Claude-Marcel Weber, 75–76
- Witnesses and reporter: Weber; the Zarauz prioress Sr Thérèse-Aimée du Cœur de Jésus and her council (signed attestation, 10 August 1910); letter of Dr Baradat, 7 September 1910
- Status: sworn testimony at the Ordinary Process; not an approved miracle
- Source: PO-19, f. 1130r–1134v and 1138v, sessions 69–70, 22–23 March 1911
- Medical or natural facts mentioned: Spring 1900: Dr Baradat of Cannes, consulted for anaemia, saw a beginning cataract. September 1900: an assistant of the oculist Abbadie (boulevard Saint-Germain, Paris) confirmed it in a dark room with electric light and said to come back in ten years for a free operation when it was "ripe". No treatment was taken after that. From 1906 he read and wrote only with difficulty, even with strong glasses; a "veil" thickened. From early 1908, in daylight, he could not recognize close friends at 12 paces; he dared not go out at dusk. May 1909: a travelling optician declared the right eye "completely extinguished" and the other very sick; Weber says he still saw a vague shadow with the right eye at two paces. Palm Sunday 1909 he fell down the choir steps in front of the parish. He feared he could no longer say Mass or his breviary. Dr Baradat's 1910 letter recalls "an opacity, a cloud" in one lens (left or right, he forgot) seen ten years before, too vague for a certificate.
- Doubts / other versions: No medical certificate before or after the cure; the promoter asked for a certificate of public knowledge instead. Cure was gradual over three novenas, not instant. Weber says symptoms began in 1899, but his first diagnosis was spring 1900. Weber dates the first improvement to March 1910 and the first novena is dated March by the prioress, though he calls it "spring". A human detail: Weber was a passionate beekeeper; he had to give up his bees; the Zarauz prioress, who had bought some colonies, wanted him to come and place an Italian queen, and said: "We are going to do violence to the good God, and he will be obliged to give you back your sight."
